    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Equation of a Level Curve",
    "problem": "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $P(1,2)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}}.$",
    "worked_solution": "First, we find the value of the function at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,2) & =\\frac{1}{2-{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $P(1,2)$ is the $1$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=1.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ 1 & =y-{x}^{2} \\\\ y & ={x}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $P(1,2)$ is the parabola $y={x}^{2}+1.$",
